A. The lanthanoids are highly reactive metals, similar to calcium. Among the choices, calcium (Ca) exhibits chemical reactivity comparable to the early lanthanoids.<br><br>B. Europium (Eu) has the electronic configuration $[Xe] 4f^7 6s^2$. The ion $\mathrm{Eu}^{2+}$ has a stable half-filled $4f^7$ configuration. However, it is a strong reducing agent because it readily loses an electron to form the more stable $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ state ($4f^6$).<br><br>C. $\mathrm{Lu}^{3+}$ has the electronic configuration $[Xe] 4f^{14}$. Since the $4f$ subshell is completely filled, there are no unpaired electrons, making the ion colorless.<br><br>D. Ytterbium (Yb) in the $+2$ oxidation state has the configuration $[Xe] 4f^{14}$. Since all electrons are paired in the $4f$ subshell, $\mathrm{Yb}^{2+}$ is diamagnetic and stable.<br><br>Answer: $\mathrm{A}: \mathrm{Ca} \quad \mathrm{B}: \mathrm{Eu}^{2+}
\quad \mathrm{C}: \mathrm{Lu}^{3+}
\quad \mathrm{D}: ~ \mathrm{Yb}$

